WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.500
Let's follow the path of Dusty's trail, oh Dusty's trail, Dusty's trail

00:07.500 --> 00:16.000
A stage and a wagon ahead and west, part of a wagon train lost from the rest

00:16.000 --> 00:23.500
Dusty's the reason for their plight, thanks to Dusty nothing's right

00:23.500 --> 00:31.000
Only the wagon master's hand keeps them a-rollin' through the promised land

00:31.000 --> 00:37.000
All that goin' gets mighty rough, but they don't seem to mind

00:37.000 --> 00:43.000
It's California, eh, that's the place they must find

00:43.000 --> 00:50.000
Deserts and rocks are in their way, but they won't stop, come what may

00:50.000 --> 00:55.000
Mountains and hills that they must scale, but they know they'll make it

00:55.000 --> 01:01.000
Cause they just can't fail on Dusty's trail

01:01.000 --> 01:19.000
Music

01:19.000 --> 01:21.000
Sure is a peaceful lookin' town, huh?

01:21.000 --> 01:23.000
Yeah, I wonder why there's nobody here

01:23.000 --> 01:25.000
I wonder why the people left

01:27.000 --> 01:29.000
Well, I'm sure they had a good reason

01:29.000 --> 01:31.000
Yeah, even if it was a bad reason, then it'd be a good reason

01:31.000 --> 01:33.000
Cause any reason for leavin' is a good reason, it's not a bad reason

01:33.000 --> 01:35.000
That's a good thing

01:35.000 --> 01:38.000
Maybe the saloon'll tell us somethin', come on Betsy

01:40.000 --> 01:43.000
Uh, shall we examine the bank, my dear?

01:43.000 --> 01:59.000
Oh my yes, that's always such great fun

01:59.000 --> 02:03.000
Nobody's been wettin' their whistle in here for quite a while

02:03.000 --> 02:06.000
Carter, look!

02:06.000 --> 02:11.000
Carter, look there through the dust

02:11.000 --> 02:13.000
What do you see, my dear?

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
More dust

02:18.000 --> 02:20.000
Well, I guess we might as well lay over here for a while

02:20.000 --> 02:22.000
Leavein' on the trail quite a spell

02:22.000 --> 02:24.000
Yeah, or even two or three spells

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
Come on, what the name of this town is?

02:26.000 --> 02:29.000
Must be on the bank, wonderin', let's go have a look

02:29.000 --> 02:31.000
Let's go have a look

02:39.000 --> 02:41.000
Puddle Flats

02:41.000 --> 02:43.000
Puddle Flats?

02:43.000 --> 02:45.000
What a peculiar name

02:47.000 --> 02:50.000
Sir, army ordinance reports that everything is ready

02:50.000 --> 02:52.000
Excellent sergeant, excellent

02:52.000 --> 02:55.000
At this time tomorrow, barring any complications

02:55.000 --> 02:59.000
The town of Puddle Flats will be blown off the face of the earth

03:13.000 --> 03:17.000
Sergeant, the army is depending heavily on the results of the test of this new explosive

03:17.000 --> 03:21.000
Ordnance feels that it is the most destructive gunpowder they've ever developed

03:21.000 --> 03:23.000
We'll know by tomorrow night, sir

03:23.000 --> 03:28.000
A squad of men will ride into Puddle Flats immediately after it's been demolished and report back at once

03:28.000 --> 03:31.000
Good, too bad about that town, sergeant

03:31.000 --> 03:34.000
Puddle Flats didn't figure to survive without water anyway, sir

03:41.000 --> 03:45.000
What's the verdict, Andy? Our water barrels are gettin' pretty low

03:46.000 --> 03:49.000
I'm sorry, Mr. Callahan, this well's as dry as a bone

03:49.000 --> 03:51.000
No wonder everybody left this town

03:51.000 --> 03:53.000
Yeah, everybody knows you can't get water from a bone

03:53.000 --> 03:55.000
That Daphne will be devastated

03:55.000 --> 03:59.000
We haven't had fresh water for tea in days

03:59.000 --> 04:00.000
Now, wait a minute, everybody

04:00.000 --> 04:03.000
You know, if I'm not mistaken about these fragments, there is water down there

04:03.000 --> 04:05.000
What are you talking about?

04:05.000 --> 04:07.000
This is rock shale, Mr. Callahan

04:07.000 --> 04:10.000
Now, the bottom of the well is merely a shallow substrat of formation

04:10.000 --> 04:14.000
Easily penetrated and in all likelihood is serving as a temporary shelf above a natural spring

04:14.000 --> 04:17.000
Wait a minute, Andy, now would you start again and give it to us in English?

04:17.000 --> 04:21.000
There's water down there

04:21.000 --> 04:23.000
Now, the people who lived in this town had known that

04:23.000 --> 04:25.000
Oh, they'd have never left

04:25.000 --> 04:27.000
Water is like gold in these parts

04:27.000 --> 04:28.000
Like gold?

04:28.000 --> 04:30.000
More valuable than gold, Mr. Brookhaven

04:30.000 --> 04:37.000
If that is really a deep spring, why, this little town could become the most important crossroads for every wagon train heading west

04:37.000 --> 04:42.000
Tell me something, how are we gonna get the water out from under that fancy rock you got there?

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
If we could shatter the outer shell of cohesive rock, we could

04:45.000 --> 04:47.000
Don't start with that again

04:47.000 --> 04:49.000
Blow it up

04:49.000 --> 04:52.000
We'll get a keg of gunpowder off the wagon, all right?

04:52.000 --> 05:21.000
All right

05:21.000 --> 05:23.000
Callahan, I got it

05:23.000 --> 05:31.000
James, Dusty, look behind you, quick

05:31.000 --> 05:36.000
Dusty, get rid of the gunpowder

05:36.000 --> 05:42.000
Get rid of it, Mr. Brookhaven

05:42.000 --> 05:48.000
Oh, Mr. Callahan

05:48.000 --> 05:55.000
That is a hip hip

05:55.000 --> 06:10.000
All right, hip hip, buddy

06:10.000 --> 06:24.000
Water, water, water, water

06:24.000 --> 06:28.000
Profitable water, my dear, and golden water

06:28.000 --> 06:33.000
Mr. Callahan, I want to call an immediate meeting of all people concerned

06:33.000 --> 06:36.000
Yes, sir, yes, sir, Mr. Brookhaven, look at that water come out of there

06:36.000 --> 06:42.000
I'm gonna go get Andy for you

06:42.000 --> 06:51.000
So, thanks to Andy's engineering experience, we have unearthed here an unending supply of water

06:51.000 --> 06:58.000
which will rejuvenate this dry, deserted little town into a budding metropolis

06:58.000 --> 07:02.000
Now then, water will bring people

07:02.000 --> 07:08.000
People will bring a railroad, and a railroad will bring enormous prosperity

07:08.000 --> 07:11.000
Here, here, I've got it

07:11.000 --> 07:13.000
That man should run for public office

07:13.000 --> 07:16.000
Mr. Brookhaven, weren't we supposed to be going to California?

07:16.000 --> 07:20.000
And so we shall, Mr. Callahan, so we shall

07:20.000 --> 07:25.000
Once we have all profited in this little liquid bonanza

07:25.000 --> 07:29.000
A liquid banana? Bonanza means like a gold mine

07:29.000 --> 07:33.000
Now then, it's really a very short-term investment

07:33.000 --> 07:36.000
with nothing to lose, everything to gain financially

07:36.000 --> 07:38.000
Who's with me?

07:38.000 --> 07:40.000
I am, Carter

07:40.000 --> 07:44.000
I knew I could count on you, my little darling

07:44.000 --> 07:46.000
What do you think, Cal?

07:46.000 --> 07:48.000
Well, I'm not against making a good investment

07:48.000 --> 07:50.000
I mean, you know, just like everybody else

07:50.000 --> 07:53.000
Oh, thank you, Mr. Callahan

07:53.000 --> 07:57.000
And once people hear about all this water, they'll flock here in hordes

07:57.000 --> 07:59.000
How many people in a horde?

07:59.000 --> 08:00.000
A gaggle

08:00.000 --> 08:01.000
Oh, a gaggle

08:01.000 --> 08:03.000
You know, Mr. Brookhaven's right about that

08:03.000 --> 08:05.000
Puddle Flats could become a big city

08:05.000 --> 08:07.000
Not Puddle Flats, please

08:07.000 --> 08:10.000
I have thought of a much better name

08:10.000 --> 08:12.000
Brookhaven, USA

08:12.000 --> 08:13.000
Brookhaven?

08:13.000 --> 08:15.000
USA?

08:15.000 --> 08:17.000
Oh, marvelous!

08:17.000 --> 08:21.000
Brookhaven, USA sounds so patriotic

08:21.000 --> 08:23.000
It sure beats the heck out of Brookhaven, Italy

08:23.000 --> 08:25.000
I think it's exciting

08:25.000 --> 08:26.000
We'll all be in business together

08:26.000 --> 08:29.000
And we shall all share in the profits

08:29.000 --> 08:31.000
Equally?

08:31.000 --> 08:34.000
Uh... uh... proportionately

08:34.000 --> 08:38.000
I shall require a small additional fee for management

08:38.000 --> 08:40.000
Yeah, well, first we all understand that

08:40.000 --> 08:42.000
Well, I think Mr. Brookhaven deserves it

08:42.000 --> 08:44.000
I mean, it was his idea

08:44.000 --> 08:47.000
Well, fair enough, you can count on little Lulu

08:47.000 --> 08:48.000
I'll fix up the saloon

08:48.000 --> 08:50.000
Yeah, I'll start collecting the well water

08:50.000 --> 08:52.000
I'll look at the schoolhouse

08:52.000 --> 08:53.000
I'll go tell Prickles

08:53.000 --> 08:55.000
Fast, wait a minute

08:55.000 --> 08:59.680
Well, Mr. Brookhaven, it looks like everybody's got their mind made up, so I guess you can

08:59.680 --> 09:01.480
count on Dusty and me too.

09:01.480 --> 09:03.080
Thank you, Mr. Cameron.

09:03.080 --> 09:10.200
And as my first official act, I appoint you Sheriff of Brookhaven, USA, and Dusty is your

09:10.200 --> 09:11.200
deputy.

09:11.200 --> 09:12.800
Oh, I don't want to be no sheriff.

09:12.800 --> 09:14.600
Oh, well, that makes me sheriff.

09:14.600 --> 09:15.600
I'll be sheriff.

09:15.600 --> 09:17.800
Dear, I feel ever so much safer.

09:17.800 --> 09:21.880
Well, Miss Brookhaven, we don't really need a sheriff here.

09:21.880 --> 09:25.520
Nobody here but just us, remember?

09:25.520 --> 09:32.600
Well, my dear, it isn't exactly the Bank of Boston, but it's ours.

09:32.600 --> 09:33.600
All ours.

09:33.600 --> 09:34.600
We share ecstasy.

09:34.600 --> 09:42.520
And when Brookhaven, USA, fulfills its promise, there will be hundreds and hundreds of people

09:42.520 --> 09:45.360
clamoring to deposit and withdraw their money.

09:45.360 --> 09:47.160
Tuck, tuck, tuck, my dear.

09:47.160 --> 09:49.200
Never think withdrawal.

09:49.200 --> 09:50.200
Always think deposit.

09:50.200 --> 09:53.200
Oh, whatever.

09:53.200 --> 09:59.040
Never mind, my dear, it was only a loose brick.

09:59.040 --> 10:08.600
Only a loose brick.

10:08.600 --> 10:10.200
How about a drink, Betsy?

10:10.200 --> 10:14.160
Oh, well, Lulu, you know I don't drink hard liquor.

10:14.160 --> 10:15.160
I know.

10:15.160 --> 10:16.160
I was just practicing.

10:16.160 --> 10:18.440
Hey, why don't you come into saloon business with me?

10:18.440 --> 10:19.840
Oh, no, thank you.

10:19.840 --> 10:21.320
I'm going to start a school.

10:21.320 --> 10:24.520
I'll tell you what, you come in with me and I'll go in with you.

10:24.520 --> 10:26.960
What could you possibly teach in school?

10:26.960 --> 10:31.480
Well, how to run a saloon.

10:31.480 --> 10:40.440
There's not even a key to the cell in here.

10:40.440 --> 10:43.160
Hey, Mr. Gallant, look what I found.

10:43.160 --> 10:46.160
The sheriff in a deputy's bed.

10:46.160 --> 10:49.800
You look just like a prisoner.

10:49.800 --> 10:50.800
I am.

10:50.800 --> 10:52.640
You locked me in, little pal.

10:52.640 --> 10:58.440
Now get the keys and get me out of here.

10:58.440 --> 11:05.400
Oh, okay.

11:05.400 --> 11:06.400
Here they are.

11:06.400 --> 11:08.400
Right under my nose the whole time.

11:08.400 --> 11:11.400
You're lucky my fist isn't right under your nose the whole time.

11:11.400 --> 11:12.400
What's that?

11:12.400 --> 11:13.400
It sounded like a gunshot.

11:13.400 --> 11:14.400
Lulu's derringer.

11:14.400 --> 11:22.640
All right, all right, what's all the excitement about?

11:22.640 --> 11:26.120
What's going on here?

11:26.120 --> 11:29.560
I didn't know where you were, so I figured you'd come and run in a gunshot.

11:29.560 --> 11:32.000
There's some very strange things going on in this town.

11:32.000 --> 11:33.000
Like what?

11:33.000 --> 11:37.280
Somebody threw a large rock at Carter and me in front of the bank.

11:37.280 --> 11:40.240
And that picture over there fell and almost killed Lulu and me.

11:40.240 --> 11:45.000
Maybe it was the same one who unplugged the keg of gunpowder.

11:45.000 --> 11:46.920
Sheriff Callahan, we demand your protection.

11:46.920 --> 11:48.600
Oh, now wait a minute, everybody.

11:48.600 --> 11:49.600
Don't get excited.

11:49.600 --> 11:52.160
Let's don't let our imaginations get the best of us.

11:52.160 --> 11:55.600
Just because this is a deserted town does not mean it's a ghost town.

11:55.600 --> 11:59.080
Well, I ain't exactly low in New York.

11:59.080 --> 12:00.880
Everybody get your hands up.

12:00.880 --> 12:03.320
I'm sorry, folks.

12:03.320 --> 12:05.640
Nobody leaves Pearl Flats alive.

12:05.640 --> 12:08.520
Greatest communique, Sergeant.

12:08.520 --> 12:13.520
We blow up bottle flats at noon tomorrow.

12:13.520 --> 12:19.600
Hey, come on now, old man.

12:19.600 --> 12:21.520
Why don't you put that gun down?

12:21.520 --> 12:22.880
Nobody here is going to hurt you.

12:22.880 --> 12:25.480
You're nothing but a lot of sidewinder and claim jumpers.

12:25.480 --> 12:27.040
What if I told you we were just leaving?

12:27.040 --> 12:29.080
Call you a liar and shoot you right between the eyes.

12:29.080 --> 12:30.880
I better think of something else.

12:30.880 --> 12:34.160
I tried to get all of you to leave, but you wouldn't.

12:34.160 --> 12:38.760
And you're the man who narrowly missed our heads with that rock.

12:38.760 --> 12:39.760
Shame on you.

12:39.760 --> 12:42.800
You could have killed us for setting fire to that keg of gunpowder.

12:42.800 --> 12:43.800
Sorry, Mothbash.

12:43.800 --> 12:45.800
Kind of scared me, too.

12:45.800 --> 12:47.800
But I'm glad you threw it in the well.

12:47.800 --> 12:49.800
Hey, look out!

12:49.800 --> 12:50.800
Hey!

12:50.800 --> 12:51.800
What a boy!

12:51.800 --> 12:52.800
Mr. Callahan.

12:52.800 --> 12:53.800
Bravo, Mr. Callahan.

12:53.800 --> 12:56.800
You captured the villain.

12:56.800 --> 12:58.520
Mrs. Brookhaven, this is not a villain.

12:58.520 --> 13:00.400
This is just an old man with some wrong ideas.

13:00.400 --> 13:03.520
All right, now you ladies take a walk.

13:03.520 --> 13:04.520
We can handle this from here.

13:04.520 --> 13:09.520
Oh, Carter, you're one of the men, too?

13:09.520 --> 13:12.520
Oh, yes, sir.

13:12.520 --> 13:14.520
Yes, sir.

13:14.520 --> 13:15.520
Sergeant.

13:15.520 --> 13:16.520
Yes, sir.

13:16.520 --> 13:20.400
Sergeant, you drew up the plans on Puddle Flats.

13:20.400 --> 13:23.200
Exactly where is the gunpowder placed?

13:23.200 --> 13:25.080
To destroy Puddle Flats completely, sir.

13:25.080 --> 13:31.000
The gunpowder has been spread underneath the saloon, the school, and under that building.

13:31.000 --> 13:32.760
What building is that?

13:32.760 --> 13:33.760
The bank.

13:33.760 --> 13:35.720
I wasn't trying to kill anybody.

13:35.720 --> 13:38.480
To scare you off my land.

13:38.480 --> 13:40.040
Distinct to claim for it, fair and square.

13:40.040 --> 13:41.040
A claim?

13:41.040 --> 13:46.360
You mean to tell me that Brookhaven, U.S. I mean, Puddle Flats is really yours?

13:46.360 --> 13:48.040
You got a paper that says that?

13:48.040 --> 13:49.040
Right here.

13:49.040 --> 13:50.040
Oh.

13:50.040 --> 13:54.760
It looks to me like he owns Puddle Flats all right.

13:54.760 --> 13:56.480
How do you do, Mr. Flats?

13:56.480 --> 13:57.720
Or should I say Puddle?

13:57.720 --> 14:00.320
Oh, what a shame.

14:00.320 --> 14:04.360
I mean, I had such get-rich-quick plans for everyone.

14:04.360 --> 14:05.980
This claim hasn't been registered.

14:05.980 --> 14:08.440
It doesn't have the notary seal of the county seat to make it valid.

14:08.440 --> 14:10.440
Oh, I'm terribly sorry, old chap.

14:10.440 --> 14:11.440
I'm really awfully sorry.

14:11.440 --> 14:12.440
Now, wait a minute.

14:12.440 --> 14:14.720
Wait a minute, Mr. Brookhaven.

14:14.720 --> 14:17.040
This old man found this town.

14:17.040 --> 14:19.480
Registration or no registration, I mean, what's fair is fair.

14:19.480 --> 14:21.600
Very well, I shall be fair.

14:21.600 --> 14:22.600
Then the town's mine.

14:22.600 --> 14:23.600
Why do you say that?

14:23.600 --> 14:29.080
How much do you want for the nebulous claim that you have to this little town?

14:29.080 --> 14:30.680
Well, let's see.

14:30.680 --> 14:36.360
Shall we take a little stroll outside and discuss the matter?

14:36.360 --> 14:47.760
If I know Mr. Brookhaven, he will have that claim before you can count to ten.

14:47.760 --> 14:48.760
Yeah?

14:48.760 --> 14:53.520
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine.

14:53.520 --> 14:56.280
That's our claim, Mr. Callahan.

14:56.280 --> 14:58.280
Why did it even get to ten?

14:58.280 --> 15:00.240
It's his claim, all right.

15:00.240 --> 15:03.120
I have an official errand for you, Sheriff Callahan.

15:03.120 --> 15:08.720
To avoid a future complication, I want you to ride forthwith and post-haste to the nearest

15:08.720 --> 15:13.920
town and register this claim properly in the name of Brookhaven, USA.

15:13.920 --> 15:21.760
And now, Andy, let us go outside and discuss this bottling plant for all this water.

15:21.760 --> 15:27.840
Well, looks like while I'm gone, you're going to be acting sheriff.

15:27.840 --> 15:29.120
Me a real sheriff?

15:29.120 --> 15:30.880
Yeah, keep your eyes open.

15:30.880 --> 15:31.880
I sure will.

15:31.880 --> 15:32.880
I'll keep one eye on the town and one eye on the people.

15:32.880 --> 15:33.880
You better keep one eye on yourself.

15:33.880 --> 15:34.880
I can't do that.

15:34.880 --> 15:35.880
I only got two eyes.

15:35.880 --> 15:36.880
I'll see you when I get back, Sheriff.

15:36.880 --> 15:37.880
Me a sheriff.

15:37.880 --> 15:38.880
A real sheriff.

15:38.880 --> 16:04.480
Sergeant, are the other cannons in position to fire?

16:04.480 --> 16:05.480
Yes, sir.

16:05.480 --> 16:26.840
I'll throw it in on the explosive underneath the bank, the school, and the saloon.

16:26.840 --> 16:27.840
I wouldn't do that if I were you, Betsy.

16:27.840 --> 16:28.840
Good morning, Deputy.

16:28.840 --> 16:29.840
Did you sleep well?

16:29.840 --> 16:30.840
It's Sheriff, not Deputy, didn't he, Slip Bowl?

16:30.840 --> 16:31.840
You better stop what you're doing.

16:31.840 --> 16:32.840
You're breaking the law.

16:32.840 --> 16:33.840
Oh, Dusty.

16:33.840 --> 16:34.840
Sheriff, you're not going to get away with this.

16:34.840 --> 16:35.840
What do you mean you're not going to get away with this?

16:35.840 --> 16:36.840
You're not going to get away with this?

16:36.840 --> 16:37.840
Sheriff.

16:37.840 --> 16:38.840
All I'm doing is filling a flower pot with dirt.

16:38.840 --> 16:39.840
What's wrong with that?

16:39.840 --> 16:40.840
It's stealing.

16:40.840 --> 16:42.840
All that dirt under the saloon belongs to Lulu.

16:42.840 --> 16:43.840
Stealing?

16:43.840 --> 16:45.320
Dusty, don't be ridiculous.

16:45.320 --> 16:47.520
What's wrong with taking a few handfuls of dirt?

16:47.520 --> 16:50.120
Because one handful leads to another handful, lots of handfuls, and lots of buckets full,

16:50.120 --> 16:51.720
and we got a big hole and the saloon falls into it.

16:51.720 --> 16:54.120
Now, you better put it all back or I'm going to have to arrest you like it says here in

16:54.120 --> 16:55.120
the book.

16:55.120 --> 16:56.560
Hey, don't be silly.

16:56.560 --> 16:57.560
You wouldn't arrest me.

16:57.560 --> 17:00.560
It was only dirt.

17:00.560 --> 17:04.280
According to the law, you're a land grabber.

17:04.280 --> 17:07.280
Well, good morning.

17:07.280 --> 17:08.280
Morning.

17:08.280 --> 17:21.880
I didn't expect to see the Army way out here.

17:21.880 --> 17:25.120
Well, we're on a special military operation, Mr. Callahan.

17:25.120 --> 17:29.480
Yeah, I'm a wagon master just on my way over here to fight a claim on a little piece of

17:29.480 --> 17:31.080
land back there called Puddle Flats.

17:31.080 --> 17:34.080
Oh, I think I can save you a trip, Mr. Callahan.

17:34.080 --> 17:37.880
Puddle Flats is a deserted town that Army Ordnance is using to test a new gunpowder.

17:37.880 --> 17:41.920
As a matter of fact, in about a half hour, our cannons will open fire.

17:41.920 --> 17:45.840
We examined that area yesterday to make certain it was completely uninhabited.

17:45.840 --> 17:46.840
It's inhabited now.

17:46.840 --> 17:48.680
You better turn those cannons of yours off.

17:48.680 --> 17:51.320
I got my wagon folks back there in Puddle Flats.

17:51.320 --> 17:53.880
Mr. Callahan, we couldn't possibly get there in time.

17:53.880 --> 17:55.840
Our cannon are over 10 miles from here.

17:55.840 --> 17:58.040
Okay, let's go, Blarney Boy.

17:58.040 --> 17:59.040
Where are you going?

17:59.040 --> 18:00.520
I'm going to get my people out of Puddle Flats.

18:00.520 --> 18:01.520
Hey, yah, yah!

18:01.520 --> 18:04.520
I thought we could make it, but we got to call off those tests.

18:04.520 --> 18:07.520
Hi, yah, Sheriff.

18:07.520 --> 18:12.520
How about a drink?

18:12.520 --> 18:15.520
No, thanks.

18:15.520 --> 18:20.520
It's on the house.

18:20.520 --> 18:22.200
I don't care where it is.

18:22.200 --> 18:23.200
I'm on duty.

18:23.200 --> 18:26.520
Well, then belly up to the bar and tell me what I can do for you.

18:26.520 --> 18:29.880
I just came to tell you I had a luck of bitzy for stealing.

18:29.880 --> 18:30.880
Bitsy stealing?

18:30.880 --> 18:32.640
Dusty, have you gone loco?

18:32.640 --> 18:35.040
She was taking handfuls of dirt right from underneath you.

18:35.040 --> 18:37.400
I mean, I caught her red handed with the black dirt.

18:37.400 --> 18:39.880
You arrested Betsy for stealing dirt?

18:39.880 --> 18:40.880
Yeah.

18:40.880 --> 18:42.880
Same kind as in the pot there.

18:42.880 --> 18:46.640
Dusty, you let Betsy out of jail or I'm going to throw this at you.

18:46.640 --> 18:47.640
You hear me?

18:47.640 --> 18:52.640
Sorry, ladies, but someday you'll thank me for making honest women of you.

18:52.640 --> 18:53.640
Ha, ha!

18:53.640 --> 18:54.640
Hey, Blarney Boy!

18:54.640 --> 18:55.640
Puddle Boy!

18:55.640 --> 18:56.640
Oh, Carter!

18:56.640 --> 18:57.640
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

18:57.640 --> 19:02.640
Another Brookhaven bank in our collection.

19:02.640 --> 19:05.640
Oh, it always gives me a tingle.

19:05.640 --> 19:06.640
Doesn't it?

19:06.640 --> 19:08.200
Not yet, my dear.

19:08.200 --> 19:10.640
I never get the tingle until I hear that jingle.

19:10.640 --> 19:14.240
Well, Mr. Brookhaven, how do you like it?

19:14.240 --> 19:15.640
Did I make your name big enough this time?

19:15.640 --> 19:19.560
Oh, the name Brookhaven is never big enough, my boy.

19:19.560 --> 19:20.560
But it will do, Andy.

19:20.560 --> 19:24.080
Well, my dear, shall we enter bank number 36?

19:24.080 --> 19:31.200
Hold it, Mr. and Mrs. Brookhaven, where do you think you're going?

19:31.200 --> 19:32.720
Why, into our bank of course.

19:32.720 --> 19:34.280
Sorry, you can't do that.

19:34.280 --> 19:36.520
And why, pray tell, may we not?

19:36.520 --> 19:38.520
Because pray tell, it's not your bank yet.

19:38.520 --> 19:42.080
Not until Mr. Callahan comes back with a registered claim, pray tell.

19:42.080 --> 19:43.560
Hold it!

19:43.560 --> 19:44.560
That's called tres...passing.

19:44.560 --> 19:45.560
You're under arrest.

19:45.560 --> 19:46.560
Under arrest?

19:46.560 --> 19:47.560
Both of you for passing tresses.

19:47.560 --> 19:48.560
Now, wait a minute, Dusty.

19:48.560 --> 19:49.560
You're under arrest.

19:49.560 --> 19:50.560
Under arrest?

19:50.560 --> 19:55.560
Of course I can.

19:55.560 --> 20:00.560
It says so right here, right next to where it says I can arrest you for obstructing justice.

20:00.560 --> 20:05.560
I don't believe it.

20:05.560 --> 20:12.560
Hey, that's my money.

20:12.560 --> 20:20.560
Hey, Dusty!

20:20.560 --> 20:23.560
Andy!

20:23.560 --> 20:26.560
Lulu!

20:26.560 --> 20:29.560
Betsy!

20:29.560 --> 20:32.560
Mr. and Mrs. Brookhaven!

20:32.560 --> 20:35.560
Where is everybody?

20:35.560 --> 20:36.560
Stand easy, boy.

20:36.560 --> 20:37.560
Hey, Dusty!

20:37.560 --> 20:38.560
Hi, Mr. Callahan, guess what?

20:38.560 --> 20:39.560
We've got no time for guessing games.

20:39.560 --> 20:42.560
We're right here.

20:42.560 --> 20:43.560
What's going on here?

20:43.560 --> 20:48.560
Dusty, come on, open up this cell.

20:48.560 --> 20:49.560
That's an order.

20:49.560 --> 20:50.560
Hey, right here.

20:50.560 --> 20:51.560
All right, now listen, everybody.

20:51.560 --> 20:52.560
We have got to get out of here.

20:52.560 --> 20:59.560
This is an army testing ground, and if we're not out of here in about two minutes, this

20:59.560 --> 21:00.560
place is going to blow to smithereens.

21:00.560 --> 21:01.560
All right, Dusty, come on, unlock this thing.

21:01.560 --> 21:02.560
Where's Dusty?

21:02.560 --> 21:03.560
What?

21:03.560 --> 21:04.560
Right behind you.

21:04.560 --> 21:05.560
What?

21:05.560 --> 21:06.560
Oh, all right.

21:06.560 --> 21:07.560
Let's get the keys unlocked.

21:07.560 --> 21:08.560
They're over there.

21:08.560 --> 21:09.560
Over here.

21:09.560 --> 21:12.560
We've got to try to save the women.

21:12.560 --> 21:14.560
Get her in the box.

21:14.560 --> 21:30.560
Maybe a few minutes, we'll get out of this alive.

21:30.560 --> 21:37.240
Fish.

21:42.940 --> 21:44.700
Got a thing we her.

21:47.360 --> 21:48.680
Please.

21:51.860 --> 21:53.600
I mean.

21:57.600 --> 21:59.300
I told him

21:59.300 --> 22:01.300
It's gone! The whole town is gone!

22:02.300 --> 22:04.300
I could have been in the saloon!

22:05.300 --> 22:06.300
We could have been in the bank!

22:06.300 --> 22:08.300
I could have been in the school!

22:08.300 --> 22:10.300
The whole town is gone except this jail!

22:10.300 --> 22:14.300
Hey, dusty little crowd! I tell you, you saved us, boy! You saved us!

22:20.300 --> 22:23.300
Thank you, dear Dusty, for arresting us!

22:23.300 --> 22:27.300
Well, one out of three is not bad.

22:30.300 --> 22:32.300
Two out of three is not bad.

22:33.300 --> 22:35.300
Three out of three is pretty good.

22:39.300 --> 22:41.300
Get it all tied down, folks.

22:41.300 --> 22:43.300
It'll be rolling as soon as I get to horses.

22:43.300 --> 22:44.300
Anybody seen Dusty?

22:44.300 --> 22:45.300
No!

22:46.300 --> 22:50.300
Sorry, Mr. Callahan. I just went back to Puddle Flats. It's just like a flat puddle now.

22:50.300 --> 22:52.300
What did you go back there for?

22:52.300 --> 22:53.300
A souvenir.

22:53.300 --> 22:54.300
Of what?

22:54.300 --> 22:55.300
Cheek.

22:56.300 --> 22:59.300
You can't wear that now. You're not a lawman anymore, Dusty.

22:59.300 --> 23:01.300
Oh, please. Just for a little while.

23:01.300 --> 23:03.300
All right. Just for a little while.

23:03.300 --> 23:04.300
Mr. Callahan?

23:04.300 --> 23:06.300
You're under arrest!

23:06.300 --> 23:07.300
What for?

23:07.300 --> 23:23.300
Nothing. I just haven't said you're under arrest for so long. I kind of missed it.

23:37.300 --> 23:39.300
You

